      "content": "The current trends in Social Capital Theory have converged on a nuanced understanding of its multifaceted nature, building upon the foundational work of Coleman (1988) and Bourdieu (1986). The concept of social capital has been expanded to encompass not only the exchange of resources but also the cultivation of trust, norms, and identity (Portes & Sensenbrenner, 1997; Lamont, 2000). The notion of \"weak ties\" (Granovetter, 1973) has been reexamined in the context of social capital, highlighting the importance of bridging relationships in facilitating collective action (Burt, 1992). This bridging capacity is distinct from bonding capital, which refers to the strong, close relationships that foster a sense of belonging and shared identity (Lin, 2001). In contrast, bridging capital relies on the exchange of resources and information across different social networks, a process that is facilitated by the structural properties of social networks, such as density and centrality (Burt, 2004). The work of Sampson and Laub (1993) has underscored the role of social capital in perpetuating and mitigating disparities, particularly in the context of urban poverty.",
